WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.678
Multiplication counts equal groups efficiently

00:00:02.678 --> 00:00:06.778
Three groups containing four dots each give twelve altogether

00:00:06.778 --> 00:00:15.433
The groups must be equal for this repeated addition model; three unequal groups would require adding their actual sizes.

00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:18.690
Division asks the reverse question

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.779
Sharing these twelve dots equally among three groups puts four in each group

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:30.433
We can also group by fours and ask how many groups fit; that version gives three groups.

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:34.639
Not every whole-number division comes out evenly

00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:39.621
Seventeen objects make three full groups of five with two left over

00:00:39.621 --> 00:00:45.433
The remainder must be smaller than five, or another complete group would still fit.

00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:50.256
The story decides what to do with a remainder

00:00:50.256 --> 00:00:55.736
If seventeen people need five-seat vehicles, three vehicles are insufficient, so we need four

00:00:55.736 --> 00:01:00.433
If we are counting complete packs, however, only three packs are full.
